ction upside down: $\\dfrac{y}{3}.$ Multiplying by the reciprocal of the divisor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x÷\\frac{3}{y} & = \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{y}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{xy}{3} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see some more examples!" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069973 #edb/ref 1065 3379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069973 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "67b283bf-2859-595a-bacb-ebf32b5f7275" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069973 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233189 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233189 #edb/ref 1069 "q-113809" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233189 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233189 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\frac{2{w}^{2}}{3}÷\\frac{w(w+2)}{7}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233189 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "97895caf-82df-5b95-8916-1e87ffb09dd3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233189 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{w}^{2}}{3}÷\\frac{w(w+2)}{7},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{w}^{2}}{3}÷\\frac{w(w+2)}{7} & = \\\\ \\frac{2{w}^{2}}{3}\\cdot \\frac{7}{w(w+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{14{w}^{2}}{3w(w+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{14{w}^{\\cancel{2}}}{3\\cancel{w}(w+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{14w}{3(w+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we canceled out a common factor of $w$ in the numerator and denominator." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272444 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{6}{g}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272444 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272444 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "06368d8d-e8cc-5093-b179-f46913edb67d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272567 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{5}{2g}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272567 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272567 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "140af3f2-c890-5051-8864-91381615cd30" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272633 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{8g}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417